<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, asymmetric (H90.3)</content>. MRI of the internal auditory canals with gadolinium is normal: no mass or enhancement in the canals or cerebellopontine angles, and normal cochleae and nerves. No retrocochlear cause was found; the cause of the loss is unknown, as it often is. Bilateral hearing aids fitted today, with real-ear measurements matching prescriptive targets to 4000 Hz, a music program with a wider input range, sound generators, and a remote microphone. [S]<list><item>Wear the aids from waking to bedtime; audiology fine-tuning in three weeks.</item><item>Musicians&apos; earplugs in loud rehearsals.</item><item>Repeat audiogram at six months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Persistent, bothersome tinnitus, bilateral (H93.13)</content>. Less intrusive with amplification and sound enrichment. [S]<list><item>Continue sound enrichment and therapy.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Adjustment disorder with depressed mood (F43.21)</content>. PHQ-9 9, from 11. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm. [S]<list><item>Weekly cognitive behavioral therapy for six more weeks, then every two weeks.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Targeted ear examination: otoscopy, tuning forks, cranial nerves</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Canals clear; aids fit well</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Comprehensive audiologic evaluation: pure-tone air and bone conduction, speech audiometry with word recognition, tympanometry, acoustic reflexes</td><td>Promptly for tinnitus with hearing difficulty; repeat at six months while progressive, then yearly and with any change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Three weeks ago; repeat at six months</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>MRI of the internal auditory canals with gadolinium</td><td>When the loss or tinnitus is asymmetric or unilateral, or with focal neurologic signs</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal; no retrocochlear lesion</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Tinnitus impact (Tinnitus Handicap Inventory, recorded in the note; no LOINC code is used)</td><td>At the start of management and when treatment changes</td><td>Due</td><td>Repeat at three months, after the aids have settled</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Tinnitus education and sound therapy</td><td>At the start and as needed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Tinnitus diary and sound machine</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy for persistent, bothersome tinnitus and the adjustment disorder</td><td>For persistent, bothersome tinnitus</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three sessions so far</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antidepressants, anticonvulsants, anxiolytics, intratympanic drugs, or supplements for tinnitus</td><td>Not recommended for routine treatment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Hearing aid evaluation, fitting with real-ear verification, and use (data logging)</td><td>Evaluation when persistent tinnitus comes with documented hearing loss; review use at each visit after fitting</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fitted today with real-ear verification</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Hearing protection for loud music</td><td>At the start; reinforce at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Custom musicians&apos; earplugs collected</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Communication access and workplace accommodation</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Remote microphone shown for one-to-one teaching; still private at work</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>At baseline and at least monthly during active treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>9, from 11</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety measure (GAD-7)</td><td>At baseline and with PHQ-9 reviews</td><td>Due</td><td>At three months</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review (PHQ-9 item 9 and structured questions since the last visit), safety plan, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Bilateral hearing aids fitted, verified, and worn at least eight hours a day by three months. In progress; target 2026-07-13 [S]</item><item>Tinnitus no longer the main barrier to work and sleep, with the Tinnitus Handicap Inventory at least 20 points lower by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 5 by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Communication access he chooses in place for rehearsals, teaching, and meetings by three months. In progress; target 2026-07-13 [S]</item><item>Hearing protection for loud rehearsals by five weeks. Outcome by 2026-05-18: Custom musicians&apos; earplugs with flat attenuation fitted with the hearing aids [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Clinical Practice Guideline: Tinnitus (Tunkel DE, et al. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2014;151(2 Suppl):S1-S40) (2014); Clinical Practice Guideline: Age-Related Hearing Loss (Tsai Do BS, et al. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2024;170 Suppl 2:S1-S54) (2024); Clinical Practice Guideline: Sudden Hearing Loss (Update) (Chandrasekhar SS, et al. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019;161(1_suppl):S1-S45) (2019); Hearing Aids (2026); Noise-Induced Hearing Loss (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Audiology fine-tuning in three weeks. Review in eight weeks. Therapy weekly. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-13: three-month review: hearing aid use, tinnitus, communication access at work, and mood. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
